From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 02:44:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 05/17] luks2: fixes from a review: layout and limit checks,
 wiping, boot identity

A second agent reviewed the code against the specification and fuzzed it
under ASan/UBSan (860,000 headers): no memory-safety bug, but:
- keyslot areas and the data segment weren't checked against each other
  (addkey could write a keyslot over data, removekey wipe a shared area):
  the keyslots area is at most 128 MiB, every area inside it and on the
  device, none overlapping, the data segment behind it;
- header values sized allocations (a 3 MiB image asked for 128 GiB):
  4000 AF stripes only, as cryptsetup; Argon2 at most 4 GiB, 64 lanes,
  2^20 passes; digests must have the hash's length and at most 10 million
  iterations; at most 16,384 JSON nodes, duplicate member names refused;
- a secondary header whose size isn't its offset is ignored; when the
  newer copy's metadata is bad, the other copy is tried;
- the boot loader frees a locked volume's JSON tree, and wipes keys when
  it reboots or returns to the firmware;
- the kernel opens the boot volume by UUID, offset and size, which the
  loader passes (an older loader's UUID alone still works);
- a 4 KiB header buffer is no longer on the (kernel) stack; AES
  temporaries and an AES-NI dead store are wiped.

craft_header.py and 8 hostile-header cases in cryptsetup_test.sh keep the
review's reproducers: 48 of 48 pass, with and without ASan.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
